<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tang Dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tang China, Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tang Dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 618 to 907 and is often regarded as a high point of Chinese civilisation. Its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> was a cosmopolitan centre linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and its institutions, law and culture influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. It interacted closely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and its collapse was followed by fragmentation and eventual reunification under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tang Dynasty" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanguole · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_outline_map,_661.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tang dynasty was founded by Emperor Gaozu, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a>, who had been Duke of Tang and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> during the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. The Li family were of ethnic Han origin and belonged to the north-western military aristocracy; Gaozu's mother was part <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>. Official Tang records trace the family's descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>, and Li Yuan used the claim to attract a following. The Tang took advantage of Sui decline and precipitated its final collapse in 618, and the first half of the dynasty was a period of progress and stability. The territory won by the military campaigns of its early rulers surpassed that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The dynasty was formally interrupted from 690 to 705, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Empress Wu Zetian</a> seized the throne and proclaimed the Wu Zhou dynasty, becoming the only legitimate Chinese female emperor. Emperor Xuanzong, who reigned from 712 to 756, ruled when the dynasty was at the height of its power.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebellion of 755–763 ended its prosperity: An Lushan, a Tang commander, rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> with more than 100,000 troops, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and named himself emperor of a new Yan state, and the court fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. The Uyghur Khaganate helped recapture the capital, but demanded an enormous tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and the Tang were never again able to reconquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>Regional military governors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a>, rose in the ninth century and weakened civil order. In the 840s Emperor Wuzong suppressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> from 874 to 884 devastated northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and sacked both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>; the Tang never recovered, and the dynasty ended in 907. It was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The dynasty kept a civil service recruited by examinations and recommendations. Emperor Taizong issued a legal code that later Chinese dynasties and neighbouring polities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> took as a model; the earliest surviving code, of 653, has 500 articles. Candidates sat the mingjing, on the Confucian classics, or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a>, which tested essays and poetry. An official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 609 counted about 50 million people.</p><p>By 742 the armies had about 500,000 men, and in 668 Tang and Silla forces destroyed the kingdom of Goguryeo. Chang'an, the capital, was laid out in 108 walled wards, and its checkerboard plan was copied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>. Over 48,900 Tang poems by more than 2,200 authors survive, among them those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bai">Li Bai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-fu">Du Fu</a>, and woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> of 868, the first full-length book printed at regular size.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tang dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baghdad sat where transcontinental trade routes linking the Mediterranean, Central Asia, the Indian subcontinent and <b>Tang China</b> met, and at its peak it was the commercial hub of the known world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Balhae arose from the wreckage of Goguryeo, the northern Korean kingdom destroyed by <b>Tang China</b> and Silla in 668.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eastern Khaganate was conquered by <b>Tang China</b> in 630 CE after the general Li Jing's surprise attack, and its ruler Illig Qaghan was captured.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>For centuries they had alternated between submission to and raids on <b>Tang China</b>, the Turks and the Uyghurs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Unified Silla, Gyeongju grew into a large city, and trade with <b>Tang China</b> and Japan flourished; in the ninth century the merchant-commander Jang Bogo controlled shipping across the Yellow Sea from his base at Cheonghaejin on Wando, and Silla communities lived in Shandong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The peace treaty Tibet swore with <b>Tang China</b> in 821–822 was carved in Tibetan and Chinese on a stone pillar that still stands in front of the Jokhang temple in Lhasa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
